  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.6,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "wrong_rating",
      "components": ["D2"],
      "pins": ["D2.1", "D2.2"],
      "nets": ["+24V", "GND"],
      "observed": "In the POWER TRAIN block, two TVS diodes sit in parallel from the +24V input node (after F2) to GND: D1 is marked 28V and D2 is marked 12V. A 12 V TVS across a 24 V rail is in continuous breakdown.",
      "expected": "The specification calls for a single 28 V TVS (D2 — SMBJ28A, '24 V-rail clamp') on the 24 V input. A clamp on the 24 V rail must have a standoff voltage above 24 V (e.g. SMBJ28A), not 12 V.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "POWER TRAIN (sheet 1): F2 7A -> +24V node with D1 28V and D2 12V both tied from +24V to GND"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "D2 — SMBJ28A TVS | Input surge/overvoltage protection on the 24 V rail | ... ('28V,' DO-214AA TVS) matches a 24 V-rail clamp"}
      ],
      "impact": "The 12 V TVS will conduct as soon as the 24 V supply rises above ~12–13 V, clamping the input rail well below its nominal 24 V, dissipating large current and almost certainly blowing the 7 A input fuse (F2). The board would fail to power up / the buck and 24 V loads would not receive 24 V.",
      "confidence": 0.6
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The schematic shows a 12 V TVS (D2) placed in parallel with the 28 V TVS (D1) directly across the +24 V input rail, contradicting the specified 28 V SMBJ28A clamp; a 12 V clamp on a 24 V rail would conduct continuously and blow the input fuse, preventing the board from powering up. Other apparent differences (LiDAR on I2C, reference-number shifts, firmware GPIO mismatches) correspond to documented design choices/known limitations and are not reported as anomalies."
}

